  "raw": {
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600772": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics (2600772) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics (2600772) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics - 2600772 __________ DEPARTMENT OF ORAL REHABILITATION FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities. Founded in 1907, the Faculty of Dentistry is based at the University’s main campus in Dunedin and is the only dental school in New Zealand. It is proud of its international reputation for research, clinical, and academic excellence. The 2026 QS World University Subject Rankings list the Dental School as 50th in the world. We are committed to working closely with iwi and Māori organisations, and mātauraka Māori has become an integral part of teaching and research across the institution. We especially value our relationship with Ngai Tahu, the mana whenua within their takiwā of Te Waipounamu, and the University’s principal Treaty partner. The role | Te mahi The Faculty of Dentistry seeks to appoint a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor in Prosthodontics. This position provides an excellent opportunity for an academic to establish themselves in one of the leading dental schools in the world. Key tasks for this position include: •    Delivering clinical teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Contributing to curriculum design for undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Supervising undergraduate and postgraduate research. •    Undertaking research resulting in quality-assured publications within the discipline, either individually or as part of a group. •    Contributing to the running of the Department, School, Division and the University. You will be supported through a collegial academic environment, an active and dynamic research culture, and through efficient professional services across the University. The Faculty is housed in modern, state-of the art facilities. Academic, research and clinical activities are centred around the Walsh Building and Clinical Services Building in Dunedin, with an additional satellite clinic in Auckland. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful applicant will hold a dental degree and a specialist qualification in Prosthodontics, preferably also a higher research degree (PhD or equivalent), and will demonstrate: •    A track record or potential of internationally peer-reviewed research. •    Teaching skills at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Ability to provide specialist patient care. •    A collaborative and collegial approach to work. •    Full understanding of the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and the University’s Māori Strategic Framework. You will be a dentist who is registerable with the Dental Council of New Zealand in the specialist scope of Prosthodontics. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (1.0 FTE) position based in Ōtepoti/Dunedin. The salary range for this position is (per annum): •    Lecturer $98,583 to $120,528 •    Senior Lecturer $148,416 to $184,993 •    Associate Professor $195,968 to $212,427 The University of Otago is known for collegiality and combined with living in the vibrant city of Dunedin, provides opportunities for an excellent work-life balance. The Dunedin campus has been ranked as one of the 15 most beautiful in the world. The city has affordable living, excellent schools, and a great array of activities, ranging from art and literature to outdoor activities. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. For further information, please contact Farai Madambi, Senior Recruitment Consultant via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV •    Cover letter •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2600772 will close on Sunday, 11 October 2026. Additional Information Contact: Farai Madambi Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Oral Rehabilitation Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600967": {
      "title": "Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland)",
      "description": "Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) - 2600967 __________ CLINICAL SERVICES FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Are you a new or recent dental graduate seeking an exciting opportunity to join a supportive culture founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility? We are looking for passionate and driven graduate dentists to join our modern team based dental practice at our facilities in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The role | Te mahi The Faculty of Dentistry invites applications for up to four full-time fixed term positions across both campuses with the possibility of three positions in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and one position in Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland, commencing 2027. In these roles you will work with experienced clinicians to provide a wide scope of general dental procedures including urgent care. Throughout this placement, you can benefit from ongoing mentoring, training, support, and coaching. These early career positions offer a unique opportunity where you can have the ability to gain valuable clinical experience under the mentorship of experienced dentists and academics within the Faculty of Dentistry, University of Otago. You can build and enhance your clinical skills in a culturally supportive environment that includes dental treatment delivering planned treatments and urgent care. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We seek Dentists within their first three years of working, who could value interacting with other professionals and who thrive on working in a supportive environment founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility. You aspire to form a firm foundation of clinical skills and there can be opportunities to get involved in teaching, research, or postgraduate learning. To be successful in this role, you should also have: •    Strong knowledge and understanding in all areas of general dentistry. •    Exceptional communication and patient care skills. •    An ability to work in a large cohesive team environment. •    Outstanding time management and organisational skills. •    A strong desire to consolidate your clinical skills in a supportive environment. •    An ability to provide high quality dental care to all age groups of patients. •    A drive to support students as a mentor in areas commensurate with your clinical expertise. •    Knowledge or the ability to acquire knowledge of procedures, policies, regulations and standards of practice. Applicants must be registered or registrable with the Dental Council of New Zealand (DCNZ). Further details | Puroko We have multiple, full-time (1 FTE), fixed term (18 January 2027 – 14 January 2028) positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin or Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The salary for these positions is $79,812 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. For further information, please contact Yolin Govender (Auckland) or Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2600967 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Yolin Govender (Auckland) Contact: Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Clinical Services Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2501589": {
      "title": "Senior Professional Practice Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry - 2501589 __________ DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities. Founded in 1907, the Faculty of Dentistry is based at the University’s main campus in Dunedin with clinics in Auckland and South Dunedin and is the only dental school in New Zealand. We are proud of our international reputation for research, clinical, and academic excellence. The 2023 QS World University Subject Rankings list the Dental School as 42nd in the world. The Faculty of Dentistry comprises of three Departments (Oral Rehabilitation, Oral Diagnostic and Surgical and Oral Sciences) as well as the Sir John Walsh Research Institute. Dunedin offers an outstanding quality of life, combining the vibrancy of a university city with the warmth of a close-knit community. The region provides exceptional schooling and educational opportunities, making it an ideal place for families. Dunedin is perfectly placed for outdoor pursuits, with world-class mountain bike trails, nearby ski fields, and excellent surfing. Just a few hours from Queenstown—the adventure capital of New Zealand—it also offers easy access to some of the country’s most stunning natural landscapes. Dunedin’s wildlife is world-renowned, home to the royal albatross, seals, penguins, and an array of native birds. ‘The outdoors and wildlife was one of our biggest pulls to this area as a family coming from the UK - also the schools are fantastic!’  H. Smith, Senior Professional Practice Fellow. The role | Te mahi The University is seeking applications from suitably qualified candidates for the position of Senior Professional Practice Fellows in Dentistry. As a Senior Professional Practice Fellow, you will help support teaching, facilitate learning, assist with assessments, and carry out clinical service/clinical activity both on undergraduate clinics and staff clinics out of term time. Some key tasks of the role include: •    Clinical teaching/tutoring of undergraduate dentists. •    Contributing to the Department’s academic goals by applying expert clinical knowledge in your area of specialty. •    Preparation, assessment, teaching and paper administration duties. •    Following through patient care and referrals within the hospital. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Successful applicants: •    Must hold a dental degree and have registration or be eligible for full registration with the Dental Council of New Zealand. •    Will demonstrate the ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels within the institution. •    Show a commitment to uphold the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and the University’s Māori Strategic Framework. Candidates will also have and maintain registration (if supervised) including: •    A practicing certificate in place before starting work. •    Indemnity in place prior to starting work. Further details | Pūroko We have eight job openings, offering both permanent and fixed-term positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. We have opportunities for Dentists with special interests in Endodontics, Prosthodontics and Periodontics. Both full-time (1 FTE) and part-time positions are available, allowing for employment over the full year or semester time only. The duration of any fixed-term positions can be negotiable with the successful candidates. International candidates would need to be available to work minimum 0.8FTE. The salary range for these positions is $109,554 to $184,993 per annum (pro rata). Candidates will be appointed to a step on the Senior Professional Practice Fellow scale 3 or 4, dependent on qualifications and skills. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children’s Act 2014.  Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. Specific enquiries about this position may be directed to Dr Pip MacDonald via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications should quote reference number 2501598. There is no formal closing date for this role. We may begin interviewing suitable candidates at any  time during the recruitment process and reserve the right to make an appointment before the advertised closing date. LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Dr Pip MacDonald Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website & Faculty of Dentistry Video Tour Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow - Medical & Dental 3 ($109,554 to $120,528) or 4 ($148,414 to $184,993) Organisation Office of the Head of Faculty (Dentistry) Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601312": {
      "title": "Senior Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine  (2601312) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine  (2601312) Senior Teaching Fellow -  Psychological Medicine - 2601312 __________ DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGICAL MEDICINE, DUNEDIN | TE TARI WHAKAORA HINENGARO TANGATA DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI The Role | Te mahi The Department of Psychological Medicine (Dunedin) advances psychological medicine through the integration of academic and clinical expertise in psychiatry, clinical psychology, and health psychology. We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to contribute to teaching within the MB ChB programme, and the Royal Australian and New Zealand College’s Formal Education Course, Registrar programme, in 2027. This position will play an important role in supporting the delivery and ongoing development of the curriculum, helping to equip future medical graduates with the knowledge, skills, and professional capabilities required to work effectively in the field of psychological medicine. The role includes convening responsibilities for the Year 6 programme and the Registrar Teaching programme, along with supporting teaching, assessment, and curriculum initiatives across the Department. Key responsibilities include: •    Convening responsibilities •    Supporting the development and delivery of course materials. •    Assisting with the administration and coordination of the MB ChB programme and Registrar programme. •    Collaborating with academic teaching and research staff •    Supporting strategic projects and initiatives that enhance teaching and curriculum development. •    Contributing to assessment activities and associated administration. •    Assisting with teaching-related operational and documentation requirements. Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako To be successful in this role, you will have: •    A medical qualification (eligible to work in NZ) as a Consultant Psychiatrist. We would consider Senior Psychiatric Registrars who are close to completing their fellowship. •    Teaching experience in the field of psychological medicine (preferable) •    Strong organisational and administrative skills. •    The ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively. •    A commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi, equity, diversity, and inclusion. Further Details | Pūroko The Department is seeking to fill a total of 0.4 FTE (15 hours per week) and is open to appointing either: •    One candidate at 0.4 FTE, or •    Two candidates at 0.2 FTE (7.5 hours per week each). This is a fixed-term position from the 7th of December 2026 for the Registrar convening role, or the 18 January 2027, for the MB ChB Year 6 convening role, to 28 January 2028, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The salary for this position will be based on the successful candidate's skills, experience, and qualifications and is expected to fall within the range of Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow Practice Fellow - $109,554 to $120,528 or $148,414 to $184,993 (pro rata). The University of Otago is a workplace that values and celebrates diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. We are committed to fostering an environment where staff from a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives feel valued, respected, and supported to succeed. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Confidential enquiries regarding the role may be directed to Associate Professor Tess Patterson via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application, please click the apply button and include your CV and cover letter. Applications quoting reference number 2601312 will close on Sunday, 18 October 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Associate Professor Tess Patterson Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow - Medical & Dental 3 ($109,554 to $120,528) or 4 ($148,414 to $184,993) Organisation Psychological Medicine Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601264": {
      "title": "College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027",
      "description": "Job Description - College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 (2601264) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 (2601264) College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 - 2601264 __________ Applications are invited for the College of Education Children's Writer in Residence for 2027 Applications are invited for the 2027 residency from children’s writers who usually reside in Aotearoa New Zealand. Begun by the Dunedin College of Education in 1992, the College of Education Children’s Writer in Residence, has allowed children’s writers to write full time in a compatible environment among colleagues who are involved with the teaching of reading and literature to children.  The financial support and imaginative space the residency afford has enabled writers to create a huge variety and number of books for New Zealand’s young readers. With support from Creative New Zealand to fund the writer’s remuneration, the residency is offered in partnership with the Robert Lord Writers Cottage Trust, which provides rent-free accommodation to writers in the historic Titan Street cottage bequeathed by the late playwright Robert Lord. What the Residency offers •    A 14-week full-time position (February 2027). •    A stipend equivalent to a University Lecturer’s salary (pro-rata), less the value of accommodation provided at the Robert Lord Writers Cottage. •    Furnished accommodation, including wireless broadband internet access at the Robert Lord Writers Cottage.  Excludes power costs. •    A dedicated writing office on campus within College of Education. •    Access to the University Library and wider University community. Who can apply Writers who are normally resident in New Zealand. A degree or formal qualification is not required. But a serious commitment to the craft needs to be demonstrated through published work or its equivalent. Early  and mid career writers are encouraged to apply. What to include Applications must include: •    Covering letter including short biography. •    Curriculum vitae. •    Details of published work & any writing awards. •    A 500-word outline of work contemplated. •    Samples of work (500 words maximum). PDF or Word files are preferred. •    Two referee reports, from referees well placed to comment on your writing experience (view the template HERE ). How to apply Applications quoting reference number 2601264 will close on Sunday, 18 October 2026. Please find links below for Conditions of Award and further information about the Residency. Additional Information Contact: Arts Fellowship Administrator Position details: Conditions of Award Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Humanities Divisional Office Job Function Fellowships ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601283": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua) in Sports Development and Management",
      "description": "Job Description -  Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua) in Sports Development and Management (2601283) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description -  Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua) in Sports Development and Management (2601283) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer (Pūkeka/Pūkeka Matua) in Sports Development and Management - 2601283 __________ SCHOOL OF PHYSICAL EDUCATION, SPORT AND EXERCISE SCIENCES TE KURA PARA-WHAKAWAI Who we are / Ko wai mātou The School of Physical Education, Sport and Exercise Sciences/Te Kura Para-Whakawai at the University of Otago/Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is internationally recognised for excellence in research, teaching and community engagement. Since 2017, it has been ranked among the world's top 25 universities for sport-related subjects in the QS World University Rankings. The School is home to an interdisciplinary community of scholars working across the natural sciences, social sciences, health sciences and humanities. We are committed to research that is internationally recognised, locally relevant, and responsive to the aspirations of Aotearoa New Zealand. The Role / Te Mahi We invite applications for a full-time, permanent Lecturer/Senior Lecturer in Sport Development and Management. The successful applicant will have expertise in Sport Development and Management informed by critical socio-cultural, policy and management scholarship. Key responsibilities include: •    Developing an impactful research programme, publishing in high-quality peer-reviewed journals, securing external research funding, and supervising postgraduate research students. •    Coordinating and delivering research-informed teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, including Sport Development and Management (SPEX209), Advanced Sport Management (SPEX314), and Advanced Topics in Sport Management and Policy (SPEX461) with contributions to the taught Master of Sport Development and Management. •    Contributing to curriculum development and academic leadership within the Sport Development and Management major. •    Contributing to the effective operation of the School through service, engagement and collegial participation within the University and wider community. •    Promoting and contributing to the University's commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and its Māori and Pacific strategic frameworks. Your skills and experience / Kā pūkeka me kā wheako To be successful in this role candidates will have: •    A completed doctoral degree in Sport Development, Sport Management, Sport Leadership, or a closely related field. •    Experience of effective undergraduate and postgraduate teaching in a tertiary environment. •    A strong publication record, commensurate with career stage, and demonstrated potential to secure competitive research funding. •    Experience supervising postgraduate research students. •    Commitment to upholding values, tikanga, kawa and traditions as guided by Ngāi Tahu, mana whenua in the region. •    Ability to build productive collaborations and work collegially. •    Practical experience supporting sport development and management initiatives within communities, including indigenous contexts, is desirable. Further Details / Pūroko This is a unique opportunity for an energetic, creative and committed academic to make a significant contribution to Sport Development and Management through teaching, research and engagement while joining a collegial and dynamic School. Sport Development and Management is a vibrant area within the School, with strengths in policy, management, sociology, coaching, psychology and history. The School values collaboration, equity and inclusion and is committed to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and the University's Māori, Pacific and Sustainability Strategic Frameworks. Applicants should be familiar with the University's strategic direction through Pae Tata: Strategic Plan to 2030. This is a permanent, full-time (1 FTE) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for a Lecturer is $97,377 to $114,943 and Senior Lecturer is $120,494 to $137,444. Specific enquiries may be directed to Professor Michael Sam, Interim Dean, School of Physical Education, Sport and Exercise Sciences via the contact details below. Application/Tono Candidates are requested to submit: •    A CV. •    A letter of application •    3-4 Referee contact details, including telephone number and email address To submit your application please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601283 will close on Sunday,18 October 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor Michael Sam Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) or Senior Lecturer / Senior Research Fellow ($120,494 to $137,444) Organisation School of Physical Education, Sport and Exercise Sciences Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601461": {
      "title": "Senior HR Adviser (Christchurch)",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior HR Adviser (Christchurch) (2601461) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior HR Adviser (Christchurch) (2601461) Senior HR Adviser (Christchurch) - 2601461 __________ HUMAN RESOURCES DIVISION | TE WĀHANGA MATUA PŪMANAWA TANGATA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has been a renowned place of learning and discovery for more than 150 years. We strive to become an even more successful, globally influential university, committed to upholding the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi as we advance outstanding research and research-informed teaching to enable transformative learning and student experiences. Our Vision/Ko te Aronga is for our people and knowledge to transform lives, connect communities and nurture the environment in Ōtākou, Te Waipounamu, Aotearoa, the Pacific, and the world. This is a rare opportunity to be part of a collaborative and dedicated HR team. We are looking for an experienced and confident HR generalist, who enjoys working independently to develop pragmatic HR solutions for clients, to join us. The role | Te mahi Working closely with the Divisional HR Manager and the Dean/Head of Region – Faculty of Medicine Christchurch, you will provide senior generalist HR advice and support to clients on our Christchurch Campus. This will involve the provision of HR technical guidance and support on a range of issues including disputes resolution, personal grievances, management of conduct and capability, change management, training and development, recruitment and selection, health and safety support and effective implementation of HR policies, procedures and best practice. You will have the support of a comprehensive HR infrastructure, and colleagues both within the HR Advisory team and across the wider Human Resources Division. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for a professional who will champion our values: Manaakitaka/Respect, Pono/Integrity, Māhirahira/Curiosity and Wharawhanaukataka/Community. You will also have: •    A tertiary qualification in a relevant field. •    Well-developed understanding of HR processes and policies. •    Recent experience in a generalist HR role. •    Excellent knowledge of NZ employment legislation and its application. •    Confidence with technology, including the ability to quickly learn and adapt to new digital tools and systems. •    Dedication to working in a culturally safe and respectful manner, including understanding and appreciation of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles, and Pasifika values. You will be effective communicator and facilitator, with proven success in building effective professional relationships. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, part-time (28 hours per week) position based in Ōtautahi, Christchurch. The University of Otago, Christchurch is the base for undergraduate medical and postgraduate health education and health research in Christchurch. We collaborate closely in our research and student programmes with the region’s hospitals run by Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora, and other community health agencies. The appointment salary range for this role is $101,739 to $110,396 per annum (pro-rata). Top of range is $119,053. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence - please contact Simone McNichol, Divisional HR Manager via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601461 will close on Sunday, 18 October 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Simone McNichol Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Christchurch Primary Location NZL-SI-Christchurch Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Management Band 2 (Range of Appointments $101,739 to $110,396, Top of Range $119,053) Organisation Human Resources Divisional Office Job Function HR Adviser ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601356": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "Job Description - Project Manager - Centre for International Health (2601356) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Project Manager - Centre for International Health (2601356) Project Manager - Centre for International Health - 2601356 __________ CENTRE FOR INTERNATIONAL HEALTH | TE POKAPŪ RANGAHAU HAUORA O TE AO DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE TAUTARINUI About the Centre | Mō te tīma The Centre for International Health is a vibrant hub for global health research, education, and collaboration within the University of Otago's Division of Health Sciences. As a key part of the Otago Global Health Institute (OGHI), a University Research Centre bringing together more than 170 researchers across disciplines, we foster innovative partnerships that address complex health challenges around the world. Working closely with collaborators in resource-limited settings, we advance research that improves health outcomes and strengthens health systems globally. Alongside our research mission, we are committed to developing the next generation of global health leaders through high-quality postgraduate training, strategic mentorship, and meaningful international engagement. The role | Te mahi We are seeking a highly organised and collaborative Project Manager to oversee the administration and delivery of a major international, multi-centre observational study within the Centre for International Health. The study is generating critical evidence to inform future Phase III vaccine trials for invasive nontyphoidal Salmonella disease, a major cause of severe illness and mortality among children in Africa. Working closely with researchers, funders, and international collaborators, you will lead all aspects of project management, including communications, stakeholder engagement, reporting and regulatory compliance, financial oversight, and the coordination of study activities. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to impactful global health research while working in a collaborative, multidisciplinary environment. You will join a team committed to research excellence, strong international partnerships, and improving health outcomes in resource-limited settings. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will be a highly motivated and adaptable individual who enjoys working both independently and collaboratively with global research partners. You will have strong project management capability, and a meticulous approach to organising information and maintaining accurate records. You will be comfortable working with complex research data, managing competing priorities, and building effective relationships across diverse international teams. To be successful in this role, you will have: •    Relevant qualifications in project management or a related field. •    Experience managing large research projects or studies. •    Excellent interpersonal skills. •    Outstanding written and verbal communication skills in English. •    The ability to quickly learn new skills and adapt to changing requirements. •    Strong problem-solving and critical-thinking skills. Further details | Pūroko This is a part-time (0.5 FTE), fixed term position for 30 months, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. Specific enquiries about the position may be directed to Professor John A. Crump, Centre for International Health via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601356 will close on Sunday, 25 October 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Professor John A. Crump Tel: +64 3 479 9460 Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Centre for International Health Job Function Research ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601500": {
      "title": "Warden",
      "description": "Job Description - Warden | Tautiaki - Cumberland College (2601500) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Warden | Tautiaki - Cumberland College (2601500) Warden | Tautiaki - Cumberland College - 2601500 __________ CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES | TE URU KAHIKA CUMBERLAND COLLEGE | TE KĀRETI O CUMBERLAND The University of Otago is renowned for its vibrant residential college communities, which support thousands of predominantly first-year students as they transition into university life. Established in 1989, Te Kāreti o Cumberland is home to 325 residents. The Tautiaki plays a pivotal role in extending manaakitanga to all members of the college community. At the heart of our kaupapa is fostering a strong sense of whanaungatanga, creating a supportive environment that underpins both the academic success and personal development of Cumberland tauira. The role | Te mahi The Tautiaki of Te Kāreti o Cumberland is a leadership role responsible for the effective and efficient provision of a collegiate experience for its tauira/residents and wider associated community. The primary role is to support our residents to reach their highest academic potential, while also providing opportunities for residents to experience cultural, sporting and personal development. We seek an experienced senior leader with proven success in dealing with the demands of a multi-faceted operation - managing people and communities. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Our ideal candidate will: •    be professionally and personally resilient and capable of handling (sometimes) difficult and complex situations, •    have demonstrated leadership, management and organisation skills, •    have the ability to perform effectively and professionally in a complex and challenging environment, including mental health and resident wellbeing related incidents, •    have experience of operating a significant budget and associated administration, •    develop effective sundry income streams including meeting rooms, hospitality, conferences, etc., •    assist in the wider division in projects and support, •    have the ability to influence and negotiate in a practical way, and •    be trusted to act in a calm manner. A minimum of a degree-level tertiary qualification is essential and experience in the tertiary sector would be a distinct advantage. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time permanent live-in position and as such you will be required to reside in accommodation, which is provided by the University.  The remuneration package includes on-call duties.  An allowance will be paid to offset the cost of accommodation. The appointment range for this role is $107,320 to $115,834. Top of range $124,348 per annum. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Vulnerable Children Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a police safety check. Confidential enquiries can be directed to Dr Sean McCallaghan, Senior Warden of Colleges, Campus and Collegiate Life Services Division. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601500 will close on Sunday, 25 October. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Dr Sean McCallaghan Tel: 021 2791130 Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Management Band 3 (Range of Appointments $107,320 to $115,834, Top of Range $124,348) Organisation Cumberland College Job Function Administrative and Operations Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601483": {
      "title": "Administrator",
      "description": "Job Description - Administrator - Student Administration (2601483) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Administrator - Student Administration (2601483) Administrator - Student Administration - 2601483 __________ ACADEMIC DIVISION | TE WĀHAKA MATUA MĀTAURAKA STUDENT EXPERIENCE Your new adventure starts here! Join the team responsible for supporting University of Otago students who represent our future New Zealand thinkers. We work to smooth the Otago journey for 21,000 students, receiving and validating applications, maintaining student records, managing the examination process, and each year supporting thousands of bright minds as they graduate from the University of Otago. We are the Student Administration team within the Academic Division. About the role | Te mahi Your tasks within the Student Administration team will depend on the specific portfolio you are supporting; this role is based predominantly in the course management team.  A full list of tasks and responsibilities can be found within the job description. Due to the nature of the role, the range of tasks and responsibilities is broad and may require performing duties at different workplaces or locations across campus. We are committed to on-going training and development in order to upskill successful candidates in areas that they desire more exposure to. Your Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for candidates with: •    Exposure to work in a busy administration environment. •    Outstanding attention to detail and accuracy. •    A passion for customer service and delivering exceptional service to students and staff. •    An ability to work independently but also collaboratively within a team environment. •    Strong Microsoft office and PC skills. Who we are | Mō te tīma Our team of professional staff focus on the experience felt by our students here at the University of Otago. Our wider Academic Division is responsible for strategic oversight of teaching and learning policy and practice, oversight of academic staff confirmation and promotion, and other various teams which provide support to our student community. Further Details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours) position, located in Dunedin. The appointment salary range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185 per annum. Top of range is $73,640. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. For further information, please contact Khazima Tahir, Lead Administrator Student Administration via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601483 will close on Thursday, 22 October 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Khazima Tahir Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Student Experience Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601357": {
      "title": "Administrator (Wellington)",
      "description": "Job Description - Administrator (Wellington) (2601357) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Administrator (Wellington) (2601357) Administrator (Wellington) - 2601357 __________ DIVISIONAL SERVICES & ADMINISTRATION | WHAKARATONGA TARI ME TE WHAKARITENGA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO, WELLINGTON | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA, PŌNEKE Who we are/Mō te tīma At the University of Otago Wellington campus, we teach medicine, radiation therapy, physiotherapy, and a wide range of postgraduate qualifications and papers, while undertaking a diverse portfolio of health-based research. We are proud to be a part of New Zealand's first medical school and have been providing New Zealand with a highly qualified health professional workforce since 1875. Our campus is supported by a team of highly skilled professional staff, working collaboratively to deliver an outstanding student and staff experience. This is achieved through the provision of high-quality administrative and operational support to our students, academic staff, researchers, and external stakeholders. About the role/Te mahi Are you an exceptional administrator who wants to play a vital role in the education of New Zealand's future health professionals and support some of New Zealand's leading health researchers? If so, this could be the opportunity for you. In these Administrator roles, you will provide proactive administrative, teaching, and timetabling support across a range of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. You will become a trusted point of contact for students, academic staff, researchers, and visitors while contributing to the successful delivery of teaching and research activities across the Wellington campus. Key responsibilities may include: •    Providing high-quality administrative support, including document preparation, minute taking, records management, and correspondence. •    Coordinating student rosters across academic and clinical learning environments. •    Supporting teaching activities, timetabling, clinical placements, and programme administration for undergraduate and postgraduate programmes. •    Coordinating student assessments and examination-related processes. •    Maintaining databases and records within University systems, producing reports and extracting relevant data as required. •    Providing administrative coordination and support to academic staff, researchers, and programme leaders. Alongside excellent administrative and organisational abilities, a strong customer service focus and the ability to build positive working relationships are critical to success in this role. Your skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for candidates who have: •    Proven experience in a busy administrative or coordination role. •    A passion for delivering exceptional service and support to customers and stakeholders. •    Proficiency in the Microsoft Office suite and confidence working with a range of systems and databases. •    Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to work collaboratively as part of a team. •    Strong organisational skills, attention to detail, and a willingness to learn. If you have outstanding organisational and administration skills and want to contribute to the successful delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate health professional education programmes, we'd love to hear from you. Further details/Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Wellington. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. The salary range for an Administrator is Level 4 ($63,508–$73,640). Candidates with Māori competency may also be considered for the Kaiwhakahaere Tari (Level 5) pathway, and candidates with Pacific cultural competency may be considered for the Administrator - Pacific Peoples (Level 5) pathway. Both Level 5 roles support the University’s Māori and Pacific Strategic Frameworks by applying Māori and Pacific values and perspectives to administrative practice and supporting student and staff success. Salary range for Level 5 roles is $69,931–$80,285. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, please contact Shan Valiyara via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601357 will close Sunday, 18 October 2026. Applications will be reviewed as submitted and candidates may be invited to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. #LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Shan Valiyara Position details: Administrator - Job Description Position details: Kaiwhakahaere Tari - Job Description Position details: Pacific Peoples - Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Divisional Services and Administration (UOW) Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601411": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Job Description - Research Technician - Surgery and Critical Care (2601411) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Research Technician - Surgery and Critical Care (2601411) Research Technician - Surgery and Critical Care - 2601411 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE, DUNEDIN | TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI •    Are you interested in the outcomes of exercise on postoperative care? •    Do you like working independently as part of a larger group? •    Do you have great interpersonal and organisational skills? •    Are you looking for part-time, flexible work? About our team/Mō te tīma The Department of Surgical Sciences excels in clinical teaching and training, linking science with clinical research, and developing our discipline nationally and internationally. Our dynamic research group, based in Dunedin Hospital, is conducting a range of innovative clinical research projects focused on improving surgical outcomes. The role/Te mahi We are seeking a motivated and detail-oriented Research Technician to support several ongoing studies. This is an exciting opportunity to work across diverse clinical areas and contribute to meaningful improvements in patient care. Key responsibilities include: •    Training and testing for a HIIT-based project aimed at improving postoperative outcomes. •    Recruit patients; schedule interviews and assist with data collection. •    Sign up participants, collect clinical data, and take rectal swabs for a colorectal surgery study. •    Enlist patients, collect urine samples, and coordinate with the K9MD team. Your skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will have some understanding of health issues, gained through relevant study or experience (e.g. as a medical, physiotherapy, or nursing student, or as a health assistant). Key will be your excellent organisational and interpersonal skills, including the ability to build rapport with patients and interact with compassion and tact. You will be able to work independently and have initiative in problem solving. •    Hold a current first aid certificate. •    Strong organisation and time management skills with meticulous record-keeping. •    Proficiency in common computer applications. •    Excellent communication skills, the ability to work both independently and as part of a team. •    Adaptability to changing research needs. •    Flexibility to work outside core hours when required. Further details/Pūroko This is a fixed-term, variable-hours position (with a guaranteed minimum of 15 hours per week) based in Dunedin, ending on 30 June 2027. Actual hours may vary depending on participant availability and project needs and will be scheduled in consultation with the successful candidate. There is scope for hours to exceed the minimum hours with the possibility of the maximum number of hours over the term of the contract being up to 30 hours a week. The appointment range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185 Top of Range $73,640 (pro rata). You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. Specific inquiries regarding the role can be directed to Dr Kari Clifford, Department of Surgical Sciences, via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601411 will close on Wednesday, 21 October 2026. Applications will be reviewed as submitted and candidates may be invited to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Dr Kari Clifford Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601492": {
      "title": "Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Classical Voice (2601492) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Classical Voice (2601492) Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Classical Voice - 2601492 __________ SCHOOL OF PERFORMING ARTS | TE KĀHUI TAU DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI About our team/Mō te tīma At the creative heart of the University, the School of Performing Arts is a vibrant and inclusive community where music takes centre stage. Our students and staff are passionate performers, composers, songwriters, educators and researchers, combining professional practice with innovative teaching and scholarship. Through our music programme, students develop their artistic voice, creative problem-solving abilities, collaborative skills and discipline-specific expertise, while gaining the confidence to express themselves and connect with audiences. Committed to excellence, we foster a supportive and respectful environment that inspires creativity, nurtures talent, and cultivates a lifelong passion for music and the performing arts. The role/Te mahi The School of Performing Arts is seeking an accomplished and passionate Classical Voice educator and performer to join our Music Programme. This is an exciting opportunity to inspire aspiring singers through high-quality individual and group teaching while contributing to a thriving artistic and academic community. As a Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow, you will provide specialist tuition in classical voice, supporting students to develop the technical, artistic and professional skills needed for successful performance careers. Working with undergraduate and postgraduate students, you will participate in audition and assessment processes, contribute to student recruitment activities, and play an active role in performances and wider School initiatives. You will also maintain your own professional practice, ensuring your teaching remains current and informed by contemporary developments in the field. As a valued member of the Music Programme, you will contribute to a collaborative and supportive environment that promotes excellence in performance and student success. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for a recognised Classical Voice practitioner with a strong commitment to teaching and mentoring emerging performers. You will bring a combination of professional performance experience, outstanding teaching capability, and the ability to engage effectively with a diverse range of students and stakeholders. To be successful in this role, you will have: •    An established professional career in classical vocal performance. •    Demonstrated excellence in one-to-one and group vocal teaching. •    Strong communication, interpersonal and relationship-building skills. •    A commitment to creating an inclusive and supportive learning environment. •    Cultural competence and an understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles and culturally safe practice. •    Registration, or eligibility to register, with an appropriate professional body such as MENZA or NEWZATS. Experience teaching at tertiary level and/or directing choirs or vocal ensembles would be advantageous. Further details/Pūroko The position is a permanent, variable hour’s role based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The hours allocated each year will depend on the number of students enrolled and their needs, which may result in appointing multiple Professional Practice Fellows. The appointment ranges for this role are Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1-2-3-4 - $75,727 to $137,444 (pro-rata). Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience and salary prorated for hours of work. You must be eligible to work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact Jennifer Cattemole, Head of School, Performing Arts via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601492 will close on Sunday, 1 November 2026. Applications will be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a variable hours position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Jennifer Cattemole Tel: +64 3 479 4025 Position details: Professional Practice Fellow JD Position details: Senior Professional Practice Fellow JD Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) or 3 ($105,665 to $114,943) or 4 ($120,495 to $137,444) Organisation Music Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601494": {
      "title": "Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Contemporary and Musical Theatre Voice (2601494) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Contemporary and Musical Theatre Voice (2601494) Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Contemporary and Musical Theatre Voice - 2601494 __________ SCHOOL OF PERFORMING ARTS | TE KĀHUI TAU DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI About our team/Mō te tīma At the creative heart of the University, the School of Performing Arts is a vibrant and inclusive community where music takes centre stage. Our students and staff are passionate performers, composers, songwriters, educators and researchers, combining professional practice with innovative teaching and scholarship. Through our music programmes, students develop their artistic voice, creative problem-solving abilities, collaborative skills and discipline-specific expertise, while gaining the confidence to express themselves and connect with audiences. Committed to excellence, we foster a supportive and respectful environment that inspires creativity, nurtures talent and cultivates a lifelong passion for music and the performing arts. The role/Te mahi As a Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow, you will provide specialist teaching across contemporary commercial music and musical theatre vocal styles. You will deliver engaging individual and group tuition, support auditions and assessments, and contribute to a dynamic learning environment that prepares students for diverse performance careers. Working closely with colleagues and students, you will help shape a vibrant performance culture through participation in productions, showcases, recruitment activities, and broader School initiatives. You will also maintain an active professional practice, ensuring students benefit from current industry knowledge and contemporary vocal pedagogy. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will be an accomplished performer and educator with expertise in contemporary and/or musical theatre voice. Passionate about developing emerging artists, you will have the skills to support students in building technical mastery, performance confidence, stylistic versatility, and industry readiness. To be successful in this role, you will have: •    An established professional background in contemporary music and/or musical theatre performance. •    Demonstrated experience teaching voice in one-to-one and group settings. •    Strong knowledge of contemporary vocal techniques and performance practice. •    Excellent communication, mentoring, and stakeholder engagement skills. •    A commitment to inclusive teaching practices and student success. •    Cultural competence and an understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles and culturally safe practice. •    Registration, or eligibility to register, with an appropriate professional body such as MENZA or NEWSATS. Experience teaching in a tertiary setting, directing vocal ensembles, or professional experience within the musical theatre industry will be highly regarded. Further details/Pūroko The position is a variable hours, fixed-term role for 3 years, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The hours allocated each year will depend on the number of students enrolled and their needs, which may result in appointing multiple Professional Practice Fellows. The appointment ranges for this role are Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1-2-3-4 - $75,727 to $137,444 (pro-rata). Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience and salary prorated for hours of work. You must be eligible to work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact the Head of Music, Jennifer Cattemole, Head of School, Performing Arts via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601494 will close on Sunday, 1 November 2026. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a variable hours position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Jennifer Cattemole Tel: +64 3 479 4025 Position details: Professional Practice Fellow-JD Position details: Senior Professional Practice Fellow-JD Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) or 3 ($105,665 to $114,943) or 4 ($120,495 to $137,444) Organisation Music Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601516": {
      "title": "Finance Associate",
      "description": "Job Description - Finance Associate (2601516) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Finance Associate (2601516) Finance Associate - 2601516 __________ FINANCE ADVISORY FINANCIAL SERVICES DIVISION (FSD) | TE RATOKA PŪTEA Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is New Zealand's oldest University and has a strong teaching and research tradition and well-established national and international linkages. It is also one of New Zealand's largest universities, with over 20,000 students, assets in excess of $2.0 billion and 4,000 full-time equivalent staff based in Dunedin, Christchurch, Wellington, Auckland and Invercargill. Ranked within the top 1% of universities in the world, the University of Otago recognises that our staff are the key to our success. The role | Te mahi As a Finance Associate, you will provide expert financial advice, analysis and day-to-day support to executives and managers that strengthen the business unit’s finances and support effective decision making. This role is part of the central Finance Advisory team of 50 staff and reports to the Finance Manager. We have two positions available, one supporting our Health Sciences Division, and the other supporting our Humanities Division. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for individuals with experience in: •    Providing financial advisory services, including month end accounting; financial reporting, analysis and commentary; financial forecasting and budgeting; financial modelling and analysis. •    Stakeholder engagement. •    Giving financial advice and guidance to non-finance senior managers. Recognised qualification(s) appropriate to the role and considerable relevant finance experience are essential for the role. A tertiary level qualification in an accounting/finance discipline is preferred. Further details | Pūroko These are full-time (37.5 hours), permanent roles located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this position is $75,631 to $80,503. Top of range $88,750 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Fraser Hunter (Divisional Manager Finance, Health Sciences) or Quentin Johnson (Divisional Manager Finance, Humanities) via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601516 will close on Sunday, 1 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Fraser Hunter - Health Sciences Contact: Quentin Johnson - Humanities Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Finance Advisory Service Job Function Accountant and Analyst ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601504": {
      "title": "Clinic Administrator",
      "description": "Job Description - Clinic Administrator - Student Health (2601504) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Clinic Administrator - Student Health (2601504) Clinic Administrator - Student Health - 2601504 __________ STUDENT HEALTH SERVICES | TE RATOKA HAUORA ĀKOKA Our team | Mō te tīma Student Health Services (SHS) provide a full range of primary health care services for students at the University of Otago. We have an experienced team of 65 full and part-time staff including GPs, Nurses, Mental Health and Wellbeing, and Administration staff who provide approximately 45,000 appointments per annum. The large and diverse health team and the distinctive campus provide a stimulating and challenging multidisciplinary work environment. Student Health Services is committed to equity and diversity and seeks to provide an accessible, respectful and welcoming environment in which all students and staff receive the individual support and resources they need. SHS promotes and upholds equity in its processes, and values the individual differences that enrich its community. We are recognised as a Rainbow Tick certified employer and a Foundation certified and Cornerstone accredited practice, which means we meet a standard of inclusivity and manaakitanga for our staff and students, and we are committed to continuous improvement. The role | Te mahi As a Clinic Administrator, you will provide effective and efficient administrative support to staff, students and patients for our Student Health Services. A strong customer service focus and confidentiality are critical to success in this role. Some key tasks include: •    Providing reception services. •    Patient registration and updating information. •    Booking appointments for students and clinicians. •    Processing referrals. •    Assisting clinicians with reporting. •    Utilising computer systems and billing. •    Undertaking external agency processes such as ACC claims registration, processing and management. •    Confidential management of patient records. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    Previous experience working in a busy, complex working environment that requires a high level of discretion, sensitivity, accuracy and interpersonal interaction. •    Previous experience working in the health sector and in particular, a medical practice would be highly advantageous (but not necessary) as would knowledge of patient management systems (Medtech Evolution or similar). •    Capable of managing multiple priorities with a strong sense of urgency. •    Demonstrate well-developed computing skills and proven competency with a range of software applications. •    Work proactively and supportively within a team to create a positive culture. •    Commitment and ability to work in a multi-disciplinary team. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, part-time (15 hours), semester only vacancy located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. Hours of work are Monday & Tuesday 12.00pm to 8:30pm. The appointment range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185 per annum. Top of the range is $73,640 (pro-rata). You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The University of Otago’s aspires to be a Te Tiriti-led university that is committed to partnership with mana whenua and upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Childrens Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. For further information or to discuss the role in confidence please contact Brylee Saunders, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601504 will close on Wednesday, 28 October 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be asked to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Brylee Saunders Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Student Health Services Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601474": {
      "title": "Communications Advisor (Māori)",
      "description": "Job Description - Communications Advisor (Māori) (2601474) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Communications Advisor (Māori) (2601474) Communications Advisor (Māori) - 2601474 __________ COMMUNICATION SERVICES | RATONGA PĀRONGORONGO EXTERNAL ENGAGEMENT DIVISION | TE RINGA TORO Who we are | Ko wai mātou He aha te mea nui ō te ao? He tangata, he tangata, he tangata! The University of Otago – Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is blessed with staff, students and community partners who are dedicated to Māori aspirations in Aotearoa. As well as celebrating Māori students, we are committed to enhancing the visibility of our teaching, research, and community engagement through a range of communications and other promotional activities. Ranked within the top 1% of universities in the world, Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has a strong national and international reputation and a firm commitment to enhance our relationship with mana whenua through our Māori Strategic Framework. Our purpose in Communications is to connect the University, internally and externally. We use a range of communications tools and channels to provide information to our audiences which: enhance and protect the University’s reputation; raises its profile; and attracts quality students and external funding. Our members are highly experienced communicators from a range of backgrounds with a strong and positive team culture. The role | Te mahi Reporting directly to the Manager Media Engagement, our Kaitohutohu Pāpāho Māori will work across the University, and promote the incorporation and actioning of the Māori Strategic Framework. This role acts as a conduit between high-level Māori staff and the wider University community and is an opportunity to inspire the next generation of Māori achievers to come learn, teach and research with us. You will proactively engage with Māori media to share stories from the University and collaborate with them to serve their audiences and promote the University of Otago’s key voices. This role has the opportunity to make a genuine difference to Māori staff and our record numbers of Māori students and highlight how Māori can impact the future world from here. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for a communications adviser who is: •    A strong writer. •    Has an in-depth knowledge of tikanga Māori. •    Has existing experience or a desire to develop a career in communications. •    Can demonstrate the skills needed for strong stakeholder engagement. •    A relevant qualification in communications, public relations, or journalism. Preference will be given to someone who has: •    Strong te reo Māori proficiency. •    Experience in communications, journalism, or public relations. You will be an articulate and engaging communicator, with a strong customer service orientation and sense of team spirit. Further details | Pūroko We are seeking to fill one permanent, part-time position (30 hours a week), based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin The appointment range for this role is $80,749 to $85,649 per annum (pro-rata). Top of the range is $100,776 (pro-rata). We offer competitive salary, 5 weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Sarah Milne, Manager Media Engagement, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application including CV and cover letter, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601474 will close on Wednesday, 28 October 2026. Applications may be reviewed as submitted. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Sarah Milne Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 7 (Range of Appointments $80,749 to $85,649, Top of Range $100,776) Organisation Director's Office, Communications Job Function Marketing and Public Relations ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601469": {
      "title": "Joint Clinical; Senior Lecturer and Consultant in Obstetrics & Gynaecology",
      "description": "Job Description - Joint Clinical; Senior Lecturer and Consultant in Obstetrics & Gynaecology (2601469) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Joint Clinical; Senior Lecturer and Consultant in Obstetrics & Gynaecology (2601469) Joint Clinical; Senior Lecturer and Consultant in Obstetrics & Gynaecology - 2601469 __________ DEPARTMENT OF OBSTETRICS AND GYNAECOLOGY | ORANGA WĀHINE FACULTY OF MEDICINE - DUNEDIN, UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO | TE KAUPEKA WHAIORA - ŌTEPOTI, ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA and HEALTH NEW ZEALAND | TE WHATU ORA SOUTHERN The Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology (O&G) | Oranga Wāhine, University of Otago, in partnership with Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora Southern, invites applications for an outstanding clinical academic to join our team in a joint clinical appointment (0.8 FTE) with clinical care focused on Early Pregnancy and Abortion Care. Drawing on the strengths of one of Australasia's leading medical schools and a progressive regional health service, this position offers a unique opportunity to influence clinical service delivery, education, research, and workforce development across Aotearoa New Zealand. The Role | Te Mahi This joint appointment combines: •    0.5 FTE Academic Component as Senior Lecturer with the University of Otago. •    0.3 FTE Clinical Component as an O&G specialist with a special interest in Abortion care with Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora Southern. As a clinical academic, you will provide leadership in teaching, research, and service development while delivering specialist clinical care with a focus on abortion and early pregnancy. You will contribute to the advancement of equitable women's health outcomes through education, research, quality improvement, and culturally safe clinical practice guided by Te Tiriti o Waitangi. Academic Responsibilities Based within the Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology | Oranga Wāhine, you will: •    Deliver high-quality undergraduate and postgraduate teaching. •    Contribute to curriculum development, assessment, and paper coordination. •    Supervise postgraduate research students. •    Develop a strong research profile through publication, external funding, and collaboration. •    Work collaboratively across the Faculty of Medicine and support the University's commitment to Pae Tata and the Māori Strategic Framework. Clinical Responsibilities Working within the Women's Health Service at Dunedin Hospital, you will: •    Provide patient-centred, evidence-based abortion and early pregnancy services. •    Deliver comprehensive sexual and reproductive healthcare, including contraception and counselling. •    Contribute to leadership, service development, quality improvement, and clinical governance. •    Support regional service provision across Otago, Southland, and wider Southern communities. •    Provide education and supervision for medical and nursing staff, while maintaining high standards of clinical care consistent with RANZCOG, Medical Council of New Zealand, and Health New Zealand requirements. Your Skills and Experience | Kā Pūkeka me Kā Wheako The successful applicant will have: •    Specialist qualifications recognised by RANZCOG (or equivalent) and recognised by the Medical Council of New Zealand. •    Eligibility for vocational registration in New Zealand. •    Experience and expertise in sexual and reproductive health, abortion care, and early pregnancy care. •    A commitment to culturally safe practice and the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi. •    Experience in undergraduate and/or postgraduate teaching. •    Demonstrated research achievement or clear potential to establish a productive research programme. •    Excellent communication, collaboration, and leadership skills. •    A commitment to quality improvement, education, and workforce development. •    The ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary teams. Desirable: A higher research degree (PhD or equivalent), experience in service leadership and clinical governance, a strong research track record, and expertise in developing sexual and reproductive health services and models of care. About Our Team | Mō Te Tīma The Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology | Oranga Wāhine is committed to advancing obstetric, gynaecological, sexual reproductive and women's health through excellence in education, research, and clinical care. We play a major role in the six-year MBChB programme and offer a range of postgraduate qualifications and research opportunities, including Masters and PhD supervision. Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora Southern serves a population of approximately 350,000 people across New Zealand's largest geographical health region. Our services are delivered through strong multidisciplinary partnerships committed to equity, innovation, and exceptional patient care. Why join us? | He aha ai mātou? This role offers the opportunity to: •    Shape the future of sexual and reproductive healthcare in Aotearoa. •    Develop a rewarding academic and clinical career within a highly respected institution. •    Work alongside nationally recognised clinicians and researchers. •    Lead innovation in early pregnancy and abortion care. •    Enjoy the lifestyle benefits of living in Ōtepoti Dunedin, a vibrant university city renowned for its natural environment, cultural offerings, and excellent work-life balance. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent joint clinical appointment (0.8 FTE total comprising 0.5 FTE academic and 0.3 FTE clinical duties) based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University of Otago and Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora are committed to equity, diversity, inclusion, and honouring Te Tiriti o Waitangi. We welcome applications from candidates who will contribute to our diverse and inclusive community. For a confidential discussion about the role, please contact Farai Madambi, Senior Recruitment Consultant, via the email below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit: •    Curriculum Vitae. •    Cover Letter. •    Contact details of three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601469 will close on Sunday, 8 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Farai Madambi Position details: Information Statement - University of Otago and Information Statement - Te Whatu Ora Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Obstetrics and Gynaecology Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601439": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Gender Studies (2601439) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Gender Studies (2601439) Teaching Fellow - Gender Studies - 2601439 __________ SOCIOLOGY, GENDER STUDIES AND CRIMINOLOGY | TE TAURA TAKATA DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI Who we are/Ko wai mātou Gender Studies within the Sociology, Gender Studies and Criminology Programme, is the largest academic unit in the School of Social Sciences at the University of Otago. We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to join our team in April 2027 in a full-time, permanent position. Gender Studies at Otago is Aotearoa New Zealand's leading programme in the field, with an interdisciplinary focus on gender and sexuality in contemporary and historical contexts. We are looking to continue our strengths in teaching gender and the media while growing into such areas as critical technology studies, critical disability and health studies. We offer undergraduate and postgraduate qualifications in Gender Studies and are committed to supporting learning through te ao Māori and Pacific worldviews. We are actively strengthening our curriculum and research in mātauranga Māori and Pacific knowledges and are committed to upholding Te Tiriti o Waitangi and the University's Māori and Pacific Strategic Frameworks. For further information please see our Gender Studies subject page: https://www.otago.ac.nz/courses/subjects/gender-studies and our Sociology, Gender Studies and Criminology Programme website: https://www.otago.ac.nz/sgc The role/Te mahi We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to join our Gender Studies team. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching across our undergraduate programme, including GEND101: Gender in Everyday Life and GEND205/305: Gender and the Media. The successful candidate will work together with Gender Studies staff, taking responsibility for coordinating papers, delivering lectures and tutorials, coordinating tutors, marking internal assessment and final exams, developing course material, paper administration and management, and coordinating with other staff members for team-taught papers. The successful candidate will have opportunities to grow Gender Studies by developing undergraduate and postgraduate teaching. The role will also include supervising postgraduate research projects, as well as providing pastoral care and course advice to Gender Studies students at all levels. Your skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will be expected to have: •    A postgraduate qualification (PhD preferred) in Gender Studies or a cognate discipline with a focus on gender. •    Demonstrated expertise in Gender & Media, as well as additional expertise in one or more areas of Gender & Disability/Health/Technology. •    A track record of teaching excellence at the tertiary level. •    Some experience of postgraduate supervision and/or mentorship. •    Evidence of an ability to work with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels of the institution. •    A commitment to contribute to teaching and learning environments that support engagement of students and staff with te ao Māori, te reo Māori, mātauranga Māori, and Pacific knowledges. •    The ability to contribute to the teaching, postgraduate and research culture, and growth of Gender Studies at Otago. Further details/Pūroko This is a full-time (1.0FTE) permanent position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range is $75,727 to $102,900 per annum (TFP1-01 to TFP2-05). Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka | University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. We welcome the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives. Specific enquiries may be directed to Associate Professor Bryndl Hohmann-Marriott, Head of Sociology, Gender Studies and Criminology via the contact details below. Application/Tono Candidates are required to provide: •    A CV, including employment history. •    A letter of application that highlights how your skills and experience address the role requirements. Please include evidence of your teaching quality in your letter. •    3-4 referee contact details, including telephone number and email address. To submit your application please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601439 will close on Sunday, 8 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Bryndl Hohmann-Marriott Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) Organisation Sociology, Gender Studies and Criminology Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601487": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant (Wellington)",
      "description": "Job Description - Executive Assistant (Wellington) (2601487) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Executive Assistant (Wellington) (2601487) Executive Assistant (Wellington) - 2601487 __________ DIVISIONAL SERVICES AND ADMINISTRATION | WHAKARATOKA ME NGĀ WHAKARITEKA Ā-WĀHAKA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO, WELLINGTON | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA, PŌNEKE •    Salary + 6.5% Superannuation + 5 weeks annual leave. •    Family friendly employer. •    Professional development opportunities. The role | Te mahi We’re looking for a proactive, experienced Executive Assistant to support our Senior Leadership Team in high-impact role. This role offers the chance to provide trusted, confidential support while keeping operations running seamlessly, perfect for a polished EA or senior administrator with a strong track record at executive level. This Executive Assistant role provides high-level, proactive support to the Dean and Head of Region acting as the first point of contact and ensuring priorities are managed effectively to support the smooth operation of a large, student-focused academic division. This role is part of the Divisional Services and Administration team, flexibly providing administrative services across the University. A strong customer service orientation and commitment to continuous improvement initiatives is critical to your success. Key responsibilities include: •    Providing a responsive first point of contact for the Dean, anticipating needs and triaging requests by urgency. •    High level diary management, correspondence, meeting administration, project assistance and coordination of campus-wide events and activities. •    Identifying and resolving issues, taking initiative to implement effective solutions. •    Preparing and reviewing high-level communications and documentation, ensuring accuracy and quality across a range of matters. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The ideal candidates bring exceptional communication skills, meticulous attention to detail, and the ability to excel in a fast-paced, constantly evolving environment. These are dynamic, multifaceted roles that require adaptability, sound judgement, and resilience. We’re seeking candidates who: •    Thrive under pressure, confidently navigating competing priorities and shifting demands. •    Work independently, taking ownership and driving tasks through to completion with minimal supervision. •    Demonstrate initiative and resourcefulness, proactively identifying improvements and resolving issues before they escalate. •    Anticipate needs, staying one step ahead and responding swiftly to emerging challenges. •    Bring energy and agility to a complex environment, contributing to a culture of excellence and continuous improvement. About our team | Mō te tīma Executive Assistant roles are part of the Divisional Services and Administration Division, a team of highly skilled professionals responsible for delivering outstanding student and staff experiences through high quality, confidential operational management, and administrative services. We are a strong supportive whakawhanaungatanga, (community), proud of the work we do as the largest workforce in the university, spanning every corner of the motu. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours), permanent position, located in Pōneke, Wellington. The appointment range for this role is $75,631 to $80,503 per annum. Top of the range is $87,750. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss this role in confidence – please contact Olivia Fuller for the role via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601487 will close on Sunday 25 October 2026. Applications may be reviewed as submitted and candidates may be invited to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Olivia Fuller Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Divisional Services and Administration (UOW) Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601567": {
      "title": "Medical Imaging Technologist",
      "description": "Job Description - Medical Imaging Technologist (2601567) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Medical Imaging Technologist (2601567) Medical Imaging Technologist - 2601567 __________ DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO The role | Te mahi Join a close-knit and highly skilled team within the Faculty of Dentistry, where your expertise will make a genuine difference to both patient care and student learning. As a Medical Imaging Technologist, you'll provide high-quality dental and maxillofacial imaging services, support the education and development of future practitioners, and contribute to research in a leading academic environment. This is an opportunity to combine your clinical skills with teaching, collaboration, and innovation, all while working in a supportive team that is committed to excellence, continuous learning, and delivering outstanding care to the communities we serve. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We're seeking a qualified Medical Imaging Technologist who combines technical excellence with a genuine commitment to patient care and collaboration. You'll be confident working in a clinical environment, enjoy being part of a supportive team, and be passionate about contributing to both service delivery and the learning experience of future dental professionals. To be successful in this role, you'll bring: •    Current registration and a practising certificate as a Medical Imaging Technologist. •    Experience producing quality diagnostic images with a strong focus on accuracy and patient safety. •    Excellent communication and interpersonal skills; able to build positive relationships with patients, students, and colleagues. •    A collaborative mindset and the ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary team environment. •    Strong organisational skills, attention to detail, and a commitment to continuous professional development. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka, University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information or to discuss the roles in confidence - please contact Nicola Sorley via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601567 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be asked to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Nicola Sorley Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Clinical Services Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601554": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Human Nutrition (2601554) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Human Nutrition (2601554) Teaching Fellow - Human Nutrition - 2601554 __________ DEPARTMENT OF HUMAN NUTRITION | TE TARI KAI TŌTIKA TAKATA DIVISION OF SCIENCE | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA Who we are | Ko wai mātou The Department of Human Nutrition at the University of Otago is the longest established department of its kind at a New Zealand University. The Department supports BSc, MSc (Thesis, Applied and Coursework options), and PhD students. Our teaching addresses research frontiers alongside real-world challenges, and our graduates are knowledgeable about the important place of nutrition in achieving social equity and supporting the wellbeing of individuals and communities. The Department has a long-standing commitment to research and teaching on the impact human nutrition has on the health and wellbeing for individuals and communities, regionally, nationally, and internationally. We aim to connect issues specific to Aotearoa New Zealand and the Pacific region with themes in the international, professional, and research discourse. The role | Te mahi We are seeking to appoint a permanent Teaching Fellow role in Human Nutrition. We welcome applicants with expertise across the breadth of Human Nutrition, including applicants with expertise in culinary nutrition, clinical nutrition and public health, and Māori and Pacific health. In this role, you will contribute to teaching across our undergraduate curriculum in Human Nutrition, including our more hands-on applied nutrition papers. You will also be involved with collaborative teaching activities, assessing student performance, and collaboratively developing course content and teaching materials. This is an opportunity for an energetic, creative and committed individual to significantly contribute to the teaching curriculum in a collegial and dynamic department and university. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    BSc (or equivalent), MSc, MAppSc or MDiet qualification in Human Nutrition. •    Enthusiasm for, and preferably experience in, research-informed teaching approaches in Human Nutrition. •    Proficiency in basic and advanced cooking techniques and ability to demonstrate safe and efficient food handling and hygiene practices. •    Understanding of recipe development and modification for nutrition goals. Skills in menu planning and meal preparation for different population groups. •    Ability to prioritise tasks, balance competing demands, navigate and collaborate effectively with a variety of working styles. •    Willingness to learn or knowledge of te ao Māori and its role in fostering student success at the University. •    Skills and commitment to strengthening curriculum in relation to te ao Māori and mātauranga Māori. •    Ability and willingness to develop a firm commitment to the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi (the Treaty of Waitangi), the University’s Māori Strategic Framework and Pacific Strategic Framework, and to equity. Some experience working with Māori and Pacific communities in nutrition and health is desirable. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full -time (1.0 FTE) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. We anticipate that the successful applicant will be able to start early February 2027, please indicate your availability in your cover letter. The salary range is $75,727 to $87,849 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. Additional enquiries about the role may be directed to Meredith Peddie, Senior Lecturer, Human Nutrition via the contact details below. Application | Tono Applications are asked to provide: •    A cover letter addressing your: -    Teaching, communication and food handling skills and experience, with a strong commitment to teaching, mentoring, and undergraduate supervision, including of Māori and Pacific students. -    Collaborative, cooperative and inclusive approach, including support for mātauranga Māori and biculturalism. •    Curriculum vitae (no more than six pages). •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. Applications quoting reference number 2601554 will close on Sunday, 22 November 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Meredith Peddie Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) Organisation Human Nutrition Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601551": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Chemistry (2601551) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Chemistry (2601551) Teaching Fellow - Chemistry - 2601551 __________ DEPARTMENT OF CHEMISTRY | TE TARI MĀTAURANGA MATŪ DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA The University of Otago is New Zealand’s 2nd largest university, with over 20,000 students and 4,000 full-time equivalent staff. Otago’s Department of Chemistry is the oldest chemistry department in New Zealand (established in 1871) and has a long-standing international reputation as a leader in research and education. There are currently 14 full-time academic staff, 10 administrative and technical staff, and 5 research staff including postdoctoral fellows within the Department. In addition, staff of the Natural Products Research Unit (part of the Bioeconomy Science Institute) work within the Department. Department of Chemistry staff teach over 550 equivalent full-time students (EFTS) including ca 2500 students taking a first-year chemistry paper. CHEM191 is a compulsory paper within of the Health Sciences First Year programme for students intending to enter professional health programmes, and is a requirement for many science/biomedical science majors. The role | Te mahi Applications are invited for a fixed-term, full-time Teaching Fellow to support teaching in undergraduate papers, primarily those at 100-level such as CHEM191 and CHEM199. In this role you will: •    Supervise or demonstrate in lab classes. •    Supervise tutorials in CHEM191/199. •    Support equity group teaching. •    Provide appropriate one-on-one and pastoral support to students. •    Support the course coordinators in the efficient running of the 100-level papers. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful applicant must have: •    A relevant tertiary qualification in Chemistry. •    A demonstrated aptitude for effective teaching at tertiary level. •    A proven ability to work well with students from different academic and cultural backgrounds in a New Zealand context. •    Excellent written and verbal communication skills, and good organisational skills. •    Commitment to supporting the University’s Māori, Pacific, Equity & Diversity, and Sustainability Strategic Frameworks. Preferred qualities for the successful applicant are: •    Tertiary postgraduate qualification in chemistry (e.g. MSc/PhD in Chemistry) •    Experience with learning management systems (e.g. Brightspace/Blackboard). Further details | Pūroko This is a fixed term (February 2027 to 30 November 2027), full-time (1.0 FTE) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin The appointment range for this role is  $75,727 to $87,849 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. We anticipate that the successful applicant will be able to start early February 2027. Please indicate your availability in your cover letter. Collaboration and collegiality are important values in the Department, and we seek a colleague who will both enjoy and enhance our cooperative work environment. Such a colleague would also be expected to adopt Kā Mātāpono/Our Staff Values of Respect, Integrity, Curiosity and Community. We are committed to equity, and we value the benefits that diversity brings to our work and our community. Specific enquiries may be directed to Professor Nigel Lucas, Head of Department, Department of Chemistry via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    Cover letter addressing the attributes under ‘Your Skills and Experience’ •    CV •    Contact details for 3 referees To submit your application please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601551 will close on Sunday, 22 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor Nigel Lucas Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) Organisation Chemistry Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601530": {
      "title": "Teaching Fellow",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Management (2601530) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Management (2601530) Teaching Fellow - Management - 2601530 __________ Department of Management | Te Mātauraka Whakahaere University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is one of New Zealand's largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked among the top 1% globally in the QS World University Rankings. The Otago Business School is AACSB- and EQUIS-accredited and recognised as one of Australasia's leading business schools, offering a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, PhD, and DBA programmes that combine research excellence with a commitment to developing graduates who can think critically and make meaningful contributions. The Department of Management | Te Mātauraka Whakahaere is a vibrant and collegial academic community committed to excellence in teaching and research. The Department delivers a broad range of programmes across management, entrepreneurship, international business, human resource management, sustainability and related disciplines. Staff are engaged in high-quality research that addresses contemporary organisational and societal challenges while contributing to national and international scholarship. The role | Te mahi The Department of Management is seeking to appoint a full-time Teaching Fellow for a three-year fixed-term period ending 30 January 2029. This role offers an opportunity to contribute to the Department's teaching activities while supporting the coordination and continuous improvement of teaching operations. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching and academic administration activities, including course delivery, assessment and examination processes, tutor coordination and development, student engagement initiatives, teaching innovation, and the enhancement of teaching practices and systems. Working closely with academic staff, programme directors, the Departmental Services Administration (DSA) team, and other key stakeholders, the Teaching Fellow will provide academic leadership and coordination across a range of teaching-related activities while helping to develop sustainable processes and resources that support teaching quality and student success. The role will also include teaching responsibilities, to be determined based on the successful candidate's expertise and departmental needs. This position will suit a highly organised and collaborative educator who enjoys working with colleagues to enhance the student experience and strengthen teaching quality within a dynamic university environment. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will have: •    A relevant postgraduate qualification in Management, Business, Higher Education, or a closely related discipline. •    Demonstrated tertiary teaching experience and a commitment to high quality student learning. •    Experience in coordinating teaching related activities, such as paper delivery, assessment, moderation, examinations, or curriculum support. •    An understanding of contemporary tertiary pedagogy and evidence based teaching practice. •    Strong organisational and project management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. •    Experience working collaboratively with academic and professional staff. •    Interest in teaching innovation, educational technologies, and the effective use of generative AI within teaching and assessment. •    Demonstrated ability to support positive student experiences and contribute to an inclusive learning environment. •    A commitment to upholding the values, tikanga, kawa, and traditions of Ngāi Tahu as mana whenua, and to working respectfully within the cultural context of the region. Further details | Pūroko This is a part-time (0.8 FTE), fixed-term position ending on 30 January 2029, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Dunedin is a vibrant university city with a strong student culture, excellent facilities, and a unique natural environment. The city offers a great lifestyle, with easy access to beaches, outdoor recreation, arts, music, restaurants, and cafés. The University of Otago is committed to Te Tiriti as set out in Pae Tata and Vision 2040. We welcome applications from candidates who bring diverse perspectives and experiences and are committed to providing an inclusive and supportive environment for all staff. Confidential enquiries about the role may be directed to Professor Sara Walton, Head of Department, Management, via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV •    A cover letter •    3 - 4 Referee contact details, including telephone number and email address To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601530 will close on Sunday, 8 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor Sara Walton Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) Organisation Management Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601538": {
      "title": "Events Supervisor",
      "description": "Job Description - Events Supervisor (2601538) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Events Supervisor (2601538) Events Supervisor - 2601538 __________ UNIVERSITY UNION | TE UNIANA O ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES | TE URU KAHIKA Who we are | Ko wai mātou Our Campus and Collegiate Life Services Division is privileged to lead a group of world class services at Otago University, dedicated to promoting the unique Otago experience and collegiate nature of the institution. The role | Te mahi This is an exciting opportunity for a hospitality professional to join one of the premium South Island employers. As Events Supervisor, you will report to the Hospitality Manager, University of Otago Union and oversee a diverse hospitality operation. Your key responsibilities will include: •    A hands-on leadership role for the operation of events across the university. •    Planning regular and varied events. •    Playing a key role in staff training. •    Ensuring that all Hospitality Services exceed the expectations of our clients. •    Building, developing and being part of an initiative-taking high performing hospitality team. •    Ensuring that all aspects of our services embrace and implements the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and the University’s Māori and Pacific Strategic Frameworks. Evening and weekend work will be required for this role. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for someone who has demonstrated leadership qualities working within a food and beverage position. You will be a strong communicator with proven ability to develop and maintain professional relationships. You will also have: •    Proven ability in performing a range of tasks under competing demands, meeting the agreed result within quality parameters and time frames. •    Attention to detail and the ability to prioritise for the needs of the business. •    Previous experience in the food service/hospitality sectors. •    Knowledge of the Sale and Supply of Alcohol Act 2012. •    Knowledge of relevant Health and Safety legislation. •    Knowledge of the Food Control Plan. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full time (40 hours) position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $35.64 to $37.14 per hour. Top of the range is $39.39. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. As part of a large organisation, we can offer you a diverse range of experiences in a dynamic professional working environment with five weeks’ annual leave, on-going professional development opportunities and a competitive superannuation scheme. For further information, please contact Adrian Lowrey, (Hospitality Manager, University of Otago Union) via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting 2601538, will close on Sunday, 1 November 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Adrian Lowrey Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Head Chef (Range of Appointments $35.64 to $37.14 per hour, Top of Range $39.39) Organisation Conference and Events Job Function Food and Hospitality Services ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601555": {
      "title": "Faculty Manager",
      "description": "Job Description - Faculty Manager - Dentistry (2601555) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Faculty Manager - Dentistry (2601555) Faculty Manager - Dentistry - 2601555 __________ DIVISIONAL SERVICES AND ADMINISTRATION | WHAKARATONGA TARI ME TE WHAKARITENGA FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO About our team | Mō te tīma The Faculty Manager role is part of Divisional Services and Administration, a team of highly skilled professionals responsible for delivering outstanding student and staff experiences. We are a strong, supportive whakawhanaukataka (community), proud of the work we do as the largest workforce in the university, spanning every corner of the motu. The role | Te mahi We are seeking a Faculty Manager to join the Faculty of Dentistry in Ōtepoti. This is a senior role leading and managing operations, ensuring all services align with the teaching, learning, research, and strategic goals of the Division. As a key member of the leadership team, you will collaborate closely with the Head of Faculty to drive strategic operational planning and function as a critical link between the departments and service units. Your leadership will be instrumental in setting high standards and maintaining strong relationships across the University. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will bring extensive experience in strategic and operational planning, reporting, and workforce analysis, supported by a demonstrated ability to lead complex change at scale. As a forward‑thinking and solutions‑oriented leader, you will apply strong diplomacy and negotiation skills to influence, guide, and align stakeholders. Your leadership will set the tone for a collaborative, high‑performing culture, inspiring teams to deliver consistently high standards across the organisation. Other key skills: •    A passion and aptitude for information precision and attention to detail. •    Technology savvy and an experienced user of Microsoft Windows and Office software. •    Able to prioritise tasks, manage time and plan to ensure delivery to deadlines. •    Can exercise sound judgement, decision making, and build productive working relationships. •    Highly professional manner and strong people skills; courteous, calm, and welcoming. •    Proven experience in business administration and project management. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. You must be eligible to work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. For further information, please contact Robyn Sutton via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601555 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Robyn Sutton Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Divisional Services and Administration (Dent) Job Function Administrative and Operations Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601545": {
      "title": "Technical Manager",
      "description": "Job Description - Technical Manager - Microbiology and Immunology (2601545) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Technical Manager - Microbiology and Immunology (2601545) Technical Manager - Microbiology and Immunology - 2601545 __________ MICROBIOLOGY AND IMMUNOLOGY | TE TARI MOROMOROITI ME TE ĀRAI MATE HEALTH SCIENCES DIVISION | TE TAUTARINUI Who we are | Ko wai mātou The Department of Microbiology and Immunology sits within the Division of Health Sciences, and we are seeking a Technical Manager to join our team in February 2027 in this full-time, permanent position. The Department has 13 research teams made up of staff and postgraduate students who undertake fundamental and applied research in microbiology, immunology and virology. We also have a larger teaching team supporting undergraduate teaching to Science and Health Science students. The Department of Microbiology and Immunology strives to create an inclusive, supportive environment to promote excellence, integrity and innovation in research and teaching. The role | Te Mahi As Technical Manager you will maintain Department facilities and equipment to allow research and teaching to continue uninterrupted, playing a vital role in the continued success of Microbiology and Immunology at Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka | University of Otago. Key tasks: •    To evaluate departmental large equipment needs including new and replacement items. •    To evaluate, remedy or request equipment and building repairs including joint oversight of building security. •    To oversee technical aspects, repairs and maintenance of a Physical Containment (PC) 2 and 3 facilities. Ongoing training will be provided. •    To ensure all laboratory equipment is in working order, including computer hardware associated with equipment housed within the building. •    To act as Deputy Health and Safety Officer and Deputy Fire Warden. •    To ensure all equipment is maintained in safe working condition. •    To be on 24hr call for the PC3 facility, monitored cold storage and the Department building and equipment. •    To communicate effectively to staff and students about repair and maintenance activities, to minimise disruption to research and teaching. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    Tertiary science qualification. •    Experience with the operating requirements for physical containment levels 1, 2, and/or 3 will be an asset. •    Strong communication skills. •    A high degree of organisation is required, including an ability to prioritise tasks and meet deadlines. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $75,631 to $80,503 per annum. Top of the range is $88,750. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. Specific enquiries may be directed to Associate Professor Jo Kirman, Head of Department via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601545 will close on Sunday, 22 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Jo Kirman Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Microbiology and Immunology Job Function Technical Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601533": {
      "title": "Adviser Communications",
      "description": "Job Description - Adviser Communications (2601533) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Adviser Communications (2601533) Adviser Communications - 2601533 __________ COMMUNICATION SERVICES | RATONGA PĀRONGORONGO EXTERNAL ENGAGEMENT DIVISION | TE RINGA TORO Who we are | Ko wai mātou Ranked within the top 1% of universities in the world, the University of Otago – Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has a strong teaching and research tradition and a well-established national and international reputation. We recognise that staff are the key to our success. The Communications Advisory Service is part of the University’s Communications Services. Our members are highly experienced communicators from a range of backgrounds with a strong and positive team culture. We’re always busy, telling great stories and supporting our inspiring staff and students as they learn, teach and research. Our purpose is to connect the University, internally and externally. We use a range of tools and channels to provide information to our audiences which enhances and protects the University’s reputation, raises its profile, and attracts quality students and external funding. The role | Te mahi Reporting directly to the Team Leader, Divisional Communications, but embedded within a client portfolio, Communications Advisers provide our portfolio leaders with expert advice and support that strengthens the University’s positive public profile and outstanding reputation, while creating and placing content for internal and external audiences. You will be responsible for providing relevant, proactive and customised communications content, advice, expert opinion and information to clients, as well as facilitating communications support from central Communications Services teams (media, internal and social) as required. This role covers the Division of Health Sciences portfolio. Each of our portfolios offers a rewarding workload due to the broad nature of the activities the University undertakes, and the proactive nature of our roles. This is a chance to work within a wider team of communications experts, working semi-autonomously under the guidance of experienced management and with great colleagues. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for an experienced communications professional, with demonstrated experience in: •    Public relations, journalism or business communications roles. •    Crafting and producing high quality communications for a range of messaging purposes. •    Providing strategic advice and support to senior leaders and managers. •    Working collaboratively with colleagues across marketing and communications functions to deliver tailored and seamless services. You will be an articulate and engaging communicator, with a strong customer service orientation and sense of team spirit. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours a week), fixed term position (December/January 2026 to July 2027) based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $80,749 to $85,649 per annum. Top of the range is $100,776. We offer competitive salary, 5 weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact Andrea Jones, Team Leader, Divisional Communications, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application including CV and cover letter, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601533 will close on Wednesday, 4 November 2026. Applications may be reviewed as submitted. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Andrea Jones Position details: Job Description Further Information: Communications Office Further Information: Division of Health Sciences Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 7 (Range of Appointments $80,749 to $85,649, Top of Range $100,776) Organisation Communications Advice Job Function Marketing and Communication ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601490": {
      "title": "Education Adviser - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow (Christchurch)",
      "description": "Job Description - Education Adviser - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow (Christchurch) (2601490) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Education Adviser - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow (Christchurch) (2601490) Education Adviser - Professional Practice Fellow / Senior Professional Practice Fellow (Christchurch) - 2601490 __________ MEDICAL EDUCATION UNIT | TE TARI WHAKAAKORANGA HAUORA FACULTY OF MEDICINE – CHRISTCHURCH | FACULTY OF MEDICINE Help shape the future of medical education. The Medical Education Unit at the University of Otago, Christchurch is seeking an experienced and collaborative Education Advisor to support excellence in learning and teaching across the medical programme. Working alongside academic staff and educational leaders, you will provide expert advice on curriculum design, assessment, course evaluation, and teaching practice. You will also contribute to academic staff development, educational innovation, quality improvement initiatives, and educational scholarship. This is an exciting opportunity to influence the learning experiences of future health professionals while working within a supportive and forward-thinking team. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will bring: •    A relevant postgraduate qualification. •    Experience in higher education teaching, learning, curriculum development, or educational evaluation. •    Excellent relationship-building and communication skills. •    Experience facilitating professional development and educational initiatives. •    A demonstrated commitment to continuous improvement in teaching and learning. •    Experience in health professional education and educational research or scholarship would be advantageous. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, part-time (0.5 FTE, 18.75 hours per week) position. The successful candidate will be appointed at either: •    Professional Practice Fellow: $75,727 to $102,900 per annum (pro-rata) •    Senior Professional Practice Fellow: $105,665 - $137,444 per annum (pro-rata) Appointment level and salary will be determined based on qualifications, skills, and experience. The position is based in Christchurch within the Faculty of Medicine and supports learning and teaching activities across the wider regional network, including occasional travel to Nelson and the West Coast. The University of Otago is committed to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and to creating an inclusive and diverse workplace. We encourage applications from candidates who will contribute to our culture of excellence in education. Specific enquiries about the position may be directed to Jeremy Simcock - Associate Dean Medical Education, via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit: •    A cover letter. •    A curriculum vitae. •    The names and contact details of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601490 will close on Tuesday, 17 November 2026. Applications may be reviewed upon receipt. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at anytime. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Jeremy Simcock Position details: Information Statement - PPF , Information Statement - SPPF and Information Statement - Addendum Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Christchurch Primary Location NZL-SI-Christchurch Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) or 3 ($105,665 to $114,943) or 4 ($120,495 to $137,444) Organisation Office of the Dean Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601590": {
      "title": "Poisons Information Officer",
      "description": "Job Description - Poisons Information Officer (2601590) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Poisons Information Officer (2601590) Poisons Information Officer - 2601590 __________ NATIONAL POISONS CENTRE | TE POKAPŪ MĀTAURANGA TĀOKE DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA About our team | Mō te tīma The National Poisons Centre is a specialised unit dedicated to providing poisoning assessment and treatment advice to the entire population of New Zealand that operates 24 hours a day, 365 days a year. It is part of the Division of Health Sciences at the University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka. The role | Te mahi As a Poisons Information Officer, you will: •    Handle telephone-based poisoning enquiries from both health professionals and the general public about acute poisoning or the toxic effects of chemicals and other substances. •    Prepare clinically oriented poisons information documents. •    Contribute to toxicovigilance and poisoning prevention activities. •    Be involved with other activities of the Centre in relation to its supply of information to various sectors of the community. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Successful candidates must possess a health science or biological science degree, preferably in pharmacology, toxicology, pharmacy, or nursing. A comprehensive knowledge of toxicology and prior clinical or healthcare-related experience would be a distinct advantage. Additional to the above, we are looking for someone who: •    Possess’ excellent communications skills, both written and verbal. •    Displays a high sense of self-motivation. •    Has established and effective computer skills. •    Has the ability to participate in a 24-hour roster including night, weekend, and public holiday work. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. Participation in a 24/7 roster (working a share of nights, weekends, and public holidays) is required, for which a salary loading applies. The salary level for this position is $75,631 to $80,503. Top of range $88,750. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The start date for the position is 11 January 2027. We offer competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave and a 6.75% superannuation scheme. Specific enquiries regarding the role may be directed to Dr Adam Pomerleau, Director, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601590 will close on Sunday 22 November 2026 Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Dr Adam Pomerleau Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation National Poisons Centre Job Function Advice, Support and Welfare ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601513": {
      "title": "Outdoor Rental Centre Lead",
      "description": "Job Description - Outdoor Rental Centre Lead (2601513) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Outdoor Rental Centre Lead (2601513) Outdoor Rental Centre Lead - 2601513 __________ STUDENT SERVICES | TE RATONGA ĀKONGA UNIPOL RECREATION | TE WHARE O TE RĒHIA The role | Te mahi Turn your passion for the outdoors into a career. We're looking for an experienced and student-focused leader to take full ownership of the University of Otago's Outdoor Rental Centre and Trotters Gorge Hut. From helping students plan their next adventure to leading a small team and overseeing a large outdoor equipment fleet, this is a unique opportunity to shape student experiences and encourage participation in the outdoors. In this role, you will: •    Take full ownership of the Outdoor Rental Centre and Trotters Gorge Hut, leading their day-to-day operation and ongoing development. •    Manage equipment, bookings, procurement, inventory, and budgets. •    Lead and develop the Outdoor Rental Coordinator and a team of student employees •    Ensure high standards of customer service, safety, compliance, and risk management. •    Identify opportunities to enhance and grow the service while promoting student wellbeing, connection, and belonging. •    Provide expert advice on equipment selection, use, and safety. Our team | Mō te tīma Based within Unipol Recreation, the Outdoor Rental Centre helps thousands of students access outdoor adventures each year. Located in one of New Zealand's premier outdoor destinations, we provide students with access to equipment, expert gear advice, and opportunities to enjoy a wide range of outdoor experiences. As the only university in New Zealand with an outdoor rental centre of this scale, we're proud to play an important role in the student experience. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will bring: •    Experience in outdoor recreation, equipment rental, recreation management, or a related field. •    A relevant qualification in outdoor recreation, leadership, or management. •    Proven leadership, customer service, and relationship-building skills. •    Strong organisational and administrative capability, including managing competing priorities. •    A commitment to health and safety, inclusion, and student wellbeing. •    A current First Aid Certificate and full New Zealand driver's licence. This is a rare opportunity to combine your love of the outdoors with a rewarding leadership role that makes a real difference to the student experience. This is someone’s dream job – if it’s yours, we'd love to hear from you! Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours) position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. You'll generally work within the Centre's operating hours of 9am–7pm, Monday to Friday, with some weekend work required during peak and busy periods. The appointment range for this role is $69.931 to $74,609. Top of range it $80,285. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. For further information, please contact Liz Campbell, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601513 will close on Sunday, 22 November 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be asked to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Liz Campbell Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 5 (Range of Appointments $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285) Organisation Recreation Services Job Function Advice, Support and Welfare ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601607": {
      "title": "Finance Associate (Christchurch)",
      "description": "Job Description - Finance Associate (Christchurch) (2601607) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Finance Associate (Christchurch) (2601607) Finance Associate (Christchurch) - 2601607 __________ FINANCIAL SERVICES DIVISION | TE RATONGA PŪTEA The role | Te mahi As a Finance Associate, you will play a key role in supporting sound financial decision-making across the University by providing expert financial advice, insightful analysis, and day-to-day financial support to executives and managers. Working as part of our central Finance Advisory team of 50 professionals and reporting to the Finance Manager, you will act as a trusted financial partner, building strong relationships with stakeholders and helping them better understand the financial implications of business decisions. We currently have an opportunity available to support a range of Health science portfolios at our Christchurch campus within Te Papa Hauora. In each area, you will serve as a key financial contact, partnering with managers to understand business needs, identify opportunities, and contribute to the overall success of the University. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are seeking a confident and adaptable finance professional who enjoys building strong relationships and partnering with stakeholders to support informed decision-making. You will bring proven experience in month-end accounting, financial reporting, analysis and commentary, forecasting and budgeting, and financial modelling. Alongside your technical expertise, you will be skilled at interpreting financial information, providing practical advice and guidance to senior non-finance stakeholders, and building trusted partnerships across a diverse range of teams and business areas. Other key skills required include: •    A relevant tertiary qualification in accounting, finance, or a related discipline. •    Significant experience in a finance advisory, management accounting, or similar financial role. •    Sound knowledge of financial management principles, accounting policies, controls, systems, and reporting requirements. •    Strong analytical and critical-thinking skills; able to interpret complex financial information and provide meaningful insights. •    Advanced proficiency in Microsoft Excel and its application in financial modelling and analysis. •    Excellent verbal and written communication skills. •    Strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. •    Exceptional attention to detail and a proactive, customer-focused approach. About our team | Mō te tīma The Financial Services Division at the University of Otago oversees all financial operations, ensuring effective management and compliance across the institution. Our team plays a vital role in maintaining financial stability and efficiency through key areas, including Financial Accounting Services, Management Accounting Services, Procurement, and Financial Systems. By providing expert financial guidance and support, we help drive informed decision-making and operational excellence across the University. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position based in Ōtautahi, Christchurch. The appointment range for this position is $75,631 to $80,503. Top of range $88,750 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka, University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Minnie Zou, Finance Manager via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601607 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Minnie Zou Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Christchurch Primary Location NZL-SI-Christchurch Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Finance Advisory Service Job Function Accountant and Analyst ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601591": {
      "title": "Officer Revenue Management",
      "description": "Job Description - Officer Revenue Management (2601591) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Officer Revenue Management (2601591) Officer Revenue Management - 2601591 __________ FINANCIAL SERVICES DIVISION | TE RATOKA PŪTEA Looking for a role that allows flexibility in your day, all while working in a small, highly effective team?   If so, this could be the role for you. The role/Te mahi As part of the Revenue Management team within Financial Control, you’ll play a key role in supporting the University’s day-to-day financial operations. The team works closely with a variety of stakeholders, providing efficient accounts receivable services and financial support across the organisation. Your responsibilities will include processing payments, managing student fees, coordinating banking transactions, issuing invoices, and following up on outstanding accounts. You’ll also help ensure financial processes and controls are followed, identify opportunities to improve systems and processes, and produce reports using financial data. A key part of the role is providing friendly and responsive support for financial enquiries, resolving issues where possible and escalating more complex matters when required. Your skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We're looking for someone who is organised, detail-oriented, and enjoys working in a fast-paced environment where accuracy matters. You’ll be a quick learner who can adapt to new systems and processes with ease, while building strong relationships with colleagues, staff, and students. The ideal candidate will be a collaborative team player who takes initiative, thinks critically, and enjoys finding practical solutions to everyday challenges. You'll thrive in a role that combines customer service, financial processing, and problem-solving. You will ideally have: •    Strong numeracy and data analysis skills. •    A passion for delivering exceptional customer service. •    Excellent organisational, time management, and problem-solving abilities. •    Proficiency in Microsoft Office and confidence learning new technologies. •    Experience in a transactional accounting environment (desirable). •    A demonstrated commitment to cultural awareness, inclusivity, and respect, including an understanding of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles, and Pasifika values. About our team/Mō te tīma The Financial Services Division at the University of Otago oversees all financial operations, ensuring effective management and compliance across the institution. Our team plays a vital role in maintaining financial stability and efficiency through key areas, including Financial Accounting Services, Management Accounting Services, Procurement, and Financial Systems. By providing expert financial guidance and support, we help drive informed decision-making and operational excellence across the University. Further details/Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for the roles is Level 4 $63,508 to $68,185. Top of Range $73,640. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. More information regarding employee benefits can be found here: Employee Benefits. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, please contact Alex Veltman, Lead Officer Revenue Management, via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601591 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Applications will be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Alex Veltman Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Revenue Management Job Function Accounts and Payroll Administration ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601603": {
      "title": "Assistant to the Deputy Secretary to the Council",
      "description": "Job Description - Assistant to the Deputy Secretary to the Council (2601603) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Assistant to the Deputy Secretary to the Council (2601603) Assistant to the Deputy Secretary to the Council - 2601603 __________ VICE-CHANCELLOR’S OFFICE | TE TARI O TE TUMU WHAKARAE The role | Te mahi Reporting to the Deputy Secretary to the Council, this is an excellent opportunity for an organised and proactive professional looking to further develop their career at the University. As part of a collaborative and highly regarded team, you will play an important role in supporting the University's governance and decision-making processes, ensuring the smooth operation of the Council, its committees, and other governance bodies. Working closely with colleagues across the University, Council members, and a diverse range of stakeholders, you will coordinate meetings, manage governance documentation, and provide high-quality secretariat support that enables effective institutional decision-making. This role offers broad exposure to senior leadership and governance activities, making it an ideal next step for someone seeking to deepen their knowledge of university operations, build strong organisational and stakeholder management experience, and grow their career within a supportive and professional environment. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We're looking for someone who is highly organised, communicates with confidence and warmth, and takes pride in delivering work to a high standard. You will thrive in a busy environment, managing multiple priorities while building effective relationships with a diverse range of people. With sound judgement, discretion, and a proactive approach, you'll confidently navigate sensitive matters and identify opportunities to improve the way things are done. This is a rewarding opportunity to contribute to the University’s governance function and support the important decisions that help shape its future. You'll also bring the following skills: •    Meeting coordination, agenda planning, and minute-taking. •    Excellent organisational and project coordination skills. •    High-level written communication and document drafting ability. •    Expertise in the Microsoft Office Suite, with the ability to quickly adapt to and navigate new systems. •    A strong customer service focus and commitment to continuous improvement. •    The ability to work proactively and collaboratively, contributing to a positive and supportive team culture. •    Committed to culturally safe and respectful practice, with an understanding and appreciation of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi and Pasifika values. Further details | Pūroko This is a part-time (20 hours per week) permanent position, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285, pro-rated per part-time hours. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss this role in confidence – please contact Jennifer Jeffery via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601603 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Jennifer Jeffery Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 5 (Range of Appointments $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285) Organisation Vice-Chancellor's Office Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601514": {
      "title": "Chief Students Officer",
      "description": "Job Description - Chief Students Officer (2601514) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Chief Students Officer (2601514) Chief Students Officer - 2601514 __________ OPERATIONS DIVISION | TE WĀHANGA WHAKAHAERE Shape the student experience at one of New Zealand's leading universities The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is one of Aotearoa New Zealand's leading universities, with a distinctive residential character and a strong commitment to student success, wellbeing and belonging. We are seeking an outstanding senior leader to join the University as Chief Students Officer, a newly established role with responsibility for the strategic leadership, integration and performance of the University's student services, pastoral care, residential life, wellbeing, safety and student experience functions. This is a rare opportunity to lead a portfolio of significant scale and breadth and to shape the services and environments that support students throughout their time at Otago. Te mahi | The role Reporting to the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Operations) and as a member of the Vice-Chancellor's Advisory Group, you will provide executive leadership across an extensive and diverse student facing portfolio. With an operating budget of approximately $107 million and more than 400 staff, the portfolio spans residential colleges and accommodation, student health and wellbeing, disability support, recreation, careers and campus safety alongside services supporting Māori and Pacific students. This is both a strategic and highly operational leadership role. Working closely with the Provost and leaders across the University, you will strengthen and connect support across the student journey, lead service improvement and redesign, and ensure services are accessible, culturally responsive, financially sustainable and easy for students to navigate. You will build strong partnerships with the Otago University Students’ Association (OUSA) and student communities, ensuring student voices inform the design and improvement of services. In this role you will also provide senior leadership for the University's pastoral care, wellbeing and student safety responsibilities, including its obligations under the Education (Pastoral Care of Tertiary and International Learners) Code of Practice and other relevant regulatory frameworks. Above all, you will help shape the unique Otago experience, creating an environment in which students can live well, feel safe, belong and participate fully in university life. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Your skills and experience We are looking for an accomplished senior leader with significant experience in the tertiary sector and a strong understanding of contemporary student experience. You will understand the diverse needs and expectations of students and how services, support and residential environments contribute to their wellbeing, belonging, participation and success. You will bring substantial operational leadership and service delivery experience across large, diverse and complex environments, together with senior level oversight of student facing services. You will know how to connect services around students’ needs and bring a strong working knowledge of pastoral care, student wellbeing and the regulatory environment within which universities operate. Commercially astute, you will be comfortable leading significant budgets and making sound decisions about priorities, resources and service models. You will have the judgement and curiosity to question how services are organised, identify opportunities for improvement, and lead change across established structures and professional disciplines. You will be an experienced people leader, who can lead through others, build capability and bring together teams with very different professional disciplines and service areas. You will also have the credibility and relationship skills to work effectively with students and student representatives, academic and professional leaders, and external partners. You will bring strong cultural capability, including an understanding of Māori and Pacific communities, te reo Māori and tikanga Māori, and a commitment to giving effect to Te Tiriti o Waitangi through your leadership. You will champion equitable outcomes and accessible, inclusive services and environments. For someone who has built a career in tertiary education and is ready for a substantially broader leadership challenge, this is a rare opportunity to strengthen Otago’s distinctive residential and pastoral model and shape the student experience at institutional scale. Pūroko | Further details This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), permanent position. Given the scale, operational nature and importance of the portfolio, the Chief Students Officer will be based on campus in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The University of Otago values diverse perspectives and is committed to creating an inclusive and respectful environment where staff and students can thrive. For further information or to discuss this role in confidence, please contact Elena Calvert (Recruitment Manager) via the email address below. Tono | Application To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601514 will close on Sunday, 15 November 2026. Additional Information Contact: Elena Calvert Position details: Information for Candidates and Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Vice-Chancellor's Office Job Function Administrative and Operations Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    }
  },
